  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Pivot long-form key-value records into a wide tabular format with distinct metric columns.\n\nPivoting long records into wide format aligns multiple experimental observations into contiguous feature vectors for ML models.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\text{df}_{\\text{wide}} = \\text{df}.\\operatorname{pivot}(\\text{index}=id, \\, \\text{columns}=metric, \\, \\text{values}=val)\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Identify row identifier index column and metric categorical header column\n2. Reshape unique metric categories into horizontal column headers\n3. Populate wide table grid with associated numeric values\n\n### Returns\nDictionary of column names mapped to list of row entries.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Unstack Long to Wide\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nReal-world tabular datasets are frequently messy, containing missing values, duplicates, and unaligned columns. Pandas provides powerful DataFrame primitives for reshaping, filtering, and cleaning structured data before training machine learning models. **Unstack Long to Wide** performs structured data manipulation efficiently.\n\nPivoting long records into wide format aligns multiple experimental observations into contiguous feature vectors for ML models. In this problem, the goal is to: pivot long-form key-value records into a wide tabular format with distinct metric columns.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\text{df}_{\\text{wide}} = \\text{df}.\\operatorname{pivot}(\\text{index}=id, \\, \\text{columns}=metric, \\, \\text{values}=val)\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Identify row identifier index column and metric categorical header column\n2. **Phase 2**: Reshape unique metric categories into horizontal column headers\n3. **Phase 3**: Populate wide table grid with associated numeric values\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Vectorized Array Operations**: Applying vectorized transformations operates on contiguous C memory buffers under the hood, running orders of magnitude faster than iterating with Python loops.\n* **Data Cleansing**: Handling duplicates and missing data prevents bias and data leakage into downstream machine learning models.\n* **Memory Management**: Choosing appropriate column dtypes (e.g. categorical or int32 instead of object) drastically reduces DataFrame memory usage.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: records = [{\"id\": 1, \"metric\": \"acc\", \"val\": 0.95}, {\"id\": 1, \"metric\": \"loss\", \"val\": 0.12}, {\"id\": 2, \"metric\": \"acc\", \"val\": 0.88}, {\"id\": 2, \"metric\": \"loss\", \"val\": 0.25}], id_col = \"id\", metric_col = \"metric\", val_col = \"val\"\n\nOutput: {\"acc\": [0.95, 0.88], \"loss\": [0.12, 0.25], \"id\": [1, 2]}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated pandas_unstack_long_to_wide under standard test conditions."
  ],
